code: 0
--- stdout
Use it once a release is tagged: it finds the issues the release includes and
applies a verb with batch = true to them, as gh dojo <verb> 12 13 14 would.
Before the tag, gh dojo release main --dry-run shows what the next one holds.

The release is the commits of <ref> (a tag, branch or commit on GitHub) since
the previous release: the highest version tag (v1.2.3) below <ref> when <ref>
is one, else the highest; prereleases (v1.2.3-rc.1) only for a prerelease.
--since names the start instead; the first release needs it. A tag or branch
is compared with <ref> on GitHub; from a commit, <ref>'s history is read down
to it. At most 1000 commits, read without a local checkout, so a branch is
named as on GitHub: main, not origin/main.

An issue is in the release when a merged pull request of a commit closes it
(its closing keywords or linked issues; squash, merge and rebase alike), or
when a commit message names it after a keyword of [release] keywords, by
default GitHub's closing ones (closes, fixes, resolves); owner/repo#N names
an issue of another repository. With [release] suffix = true, so do the
parentheses that end a message's first line: "fix: login limit (#12)",
"(#12, #13)". Code in a message (`Refs #12`, a ``` block)
does not count: put examples of the syntax in backticks.

A message names issues as text, so a number only messages name that is no
issue (a pull request, a missing issue, or one of a repository outside
board.repos that is not found or the token cannot see) is left out with a
warning, not failed. --exclude leaves out any issue found, however it was
found.

An open issue whose status the verb's from does not allow (still in progress,
say) is listed as not ready and left out, not failed; only a later release
whose commits name it again picks it up. A closed one is refused (exit code 2),
unless it is in a final status (statuses.final): then it is done with, listed as
already closed and left out, and the verb does not touch it.

--dry-run lists each issue with how it was found and its plan.

Usage:
  gh dojo release <ref> --verb <verb> [flags]

Examples:
  gh dojo release main --verb done --dry-run
  gh dojo release v0.2.0 --verb done --dry-run
  gh dojo release v0.2.0 --verb done
  gh dojo release v0.2.0 --verb done --exclude acme/api#3
  gh dojo release v0.1.0 --since 4c40894 --verb done --comment "Released in v0.1.0."

Flags:
      --by string        value for {by} in the verb's comment, for every issue
      --comment string   text for {comment} in the verb's comment, for every issue
      --dry-run          list the issues, how each was found and its plan, without changing anything
      --exclude issue    an issue found to leave out, by a pull request or a message: 12, "#12", owner/repo#12 or its URL; repeatable, or comma-separated
  -h, --help             help for release
      --since string     where the release starts, a tag, branch or commit; default: the previous version tag
      --verb string      the verb to apply to the release's issues: one with batch = true
      --yes              the user agreed: for a verb that is the user's decision

Global Flags:
  -q, --jq expression       filter the JSON with a jq expression (implies --json)
      --json                print the result as JSON (schema_version 1), errors too
  -t, --template template   format the JSON with a Go template (implies --json)
